i used to be good at root cause analysis
it was so fun to set logs and breakpoints and feel the rush of the aha! moment when you figured out what caused a bug, especially when you were the first on the team to repro a tricky one

i used to be good at keeping codebases organized
i could build and hold big maps of property references in my head. i could trace their paths through the codebase and decide when a spaghetti-like path needed to be snapped into something simpler. i wrote DRY, organized classes that told a story as you read them top to bottom.

i used to be good at memory management
i used to use the memory profiler in visual studio. i used to be able to trace big spikes in memory usage; i knew how to find a little nit reference that was preventing an object from being disposed and i used to take great pleasure in eviscerating it. i used to know more about how the garbage collector worked; when it would run. i used to be militant about writing an iDisposable implementation and I knew the order in which things had to be disposed.

i used to be good at code reviews
i had a 1-1 map of the codebase in my head so I could tell at a glance when something didn't match our established pattern. I would find bugs before the code holding them got merged. if i wasn't sure, i'd pull my coworker's code and inspect it in my ide or run it myself. i still do this sometimes, but much less than i used to.

i don't think i was ever particularly good at unit tests
boring! but it was satisfying to watch code coverage go up and write a suite that my tech lead approved of
